From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SplitPlay <splitplay@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 21:14:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] splitplay: probe HDR on the desk session's displays

A desk's display is Microsoft's RdpIdd. Windows turns HDR on for it only
when the RDP client asks for HDR over the GFX pipeline, and the seat
connects without GFX, so a desk is expected to be SDR only. This patch
measures that instead of assuming it.

splitplay::hdr::probe() reads every display the session sees: DXGI gives
the output's colour space (HDR10 is what display_base_t::is_hdr() checks
before a stream is encoded as HDR), its bits per colour and peak
luminance; the Display Configuration API gives whether the display
supports HDR and whether it is on (DISPLAYCONFIG_GET_ADVANCED_COLOR_INFO_2
on Windows 11 24H2, the older advanced colour info otherwise; both
structures are declared locally since older MinGW headers lack the 24H2
one). The state is "enabled", "off" (supported, switched off),
"unsupported" or "unknown". Before 24H2 an SDR display that could use
auto colour management looks like an HDR display with HDR off, so there
"supported" is left unknown unless HDR is on or there is no advanced
colour at all.

- A thread of its own probes at start and every 5 s (DXGI and the
  Display Configuration API can stall during a mode change, and a stalled
  heartbeat thread gets the worker killed); the heartbeat thread sends
  `event display_hdr` with the probe whenever it differs from the last
  one, so the service always knows the desk's HDR state.
- --self-test ends with hdr::try_enable(): it switches HDR on for every
  display whose HDR is off by both accounts (DISPLAYCONFIG_SET_HDR_STATE
  where the 24H2 info answered, the older SET_ADVANCED_COLOR_STATE
  otherwise), probes again two seconds later and switches each changed
  display back with the same call. self-test.json gets "hdr" with before, after,
  each attempt's Win32 result and "ok" (a display composed in HDR10 once
  asked). It does not change the self-test's own result.
- A stream whose client asked for HDR on a display that is not in HDR
  logs a warning with the probe's state: Sunshine then encodes SDR in 10
  bits, as upstream does.

